{"combines": true, "proposition": "Together, the SEM morphology and BIO platelet-count data show that the BVLD/NO-PPAmF surface has both the lowest quantitative platelet adhesion (~5x1e2 cells/mm2, the lowest of the five surfaces: 316L SS ~32, PPAmF ~41, BVLD-PPAmF ~15.5, NO-PPAmF ~11, BVLD/NO-PPAmF ~5) and the least activated platelet morphology (a few round, unspread platelets rather than the many spread, dendritic platelets seen on 316L SS and PPAmF, or the intermediate numbers on BVLD-PPAmF/NO-PPAmF). Combining the two shows suppression on two distinct dimensions at once -- adhesion (a count) and activation state (a morphology) -- for the same surface, which neither measurement conveys by itself: SEM alone gives only relative, qualitative morphology/density impressions without a precise cross-surface quantitative ranking, and BIO alone gives only counts without any information on whether adhered platelets are activated (spread/dendritic) or not (round/unspread).", "limits": ["Does not establish a causal mechanism for why the BVLD/NO-PPAmF combination outperforms BVLD-PPAmF or NO-PPAmF alone", "Does not establish statistical significance of the count differences (no replicate/error information given)", "Cannot separate whether the low BIO count on BVLD/NO-PPAmF reflects genuinely reduced initial adhesion, or whether it partly reflects preferential detachment/washout of activated (spread) platelets during sample processing, since spread platelets typically adhere more firmly than round ones -- an activation-dependent retention confound", "The SEM categories (many/fewer/few, spread vs round) are not point-matched to the specific BIO count values; the two observations run in parallel on the same surfaces rather than being mathematically correlated", "Does not separate the individual contribution of BVLD-PPAmF versus NO-PPAmF to the combined surface's lower values"], "not_identifiable": "true suppression of platelet adhesion on BVLD/NO-PPAmF vs. activation-dependent 